at was  all going to work out my job ran the  spectrum it was a really small staff so  we all wore lots of hats so my job has  everything from meeting a world leader  at the front of the office bringing them  in making sure the meeting was going  well snapping photographs of their  meeting  bringing in their lunch and at the end  maybe even clearing the dishes he kind  of did a little bit of everything I had  to make sure he had cash in his pocket  when he wanted to get his haircut  presidents don't always carry a lot of  cash around make sure he had toothpaste  in his bathroom if he wanted to brush  his teeth and sharpen pencils at his  desk so the job ran the spectrum  anything and everything he made it I  tried to take care of it for him and in  fact tried to anticipate so that he  didn't even have to ask sandy him behind  the scenes I realized that there were  not two Ronald Reagan's exactly who you  saw publicly it was exactly who he was  privately so when we were traveling we  were just an event in a backstage hold  area the same graciousness and warmth  that he put on for the camera was the  same way he's left his like behind the  scenes what a wonderful example of  character and what a great example for  all of us to remember especially in a  leadership role there's always people  around you watching and whether or not  he was conscious of that he was very  much the same person all the time behind  the scenes I loved working for him of  course it was a very difficult very  fast-paced job but what a joy he made it  to work for him  there's so many things I look back on  and you know all the glamorous  star-studded events but it's kind of  ironic that when I look back on what  were my favorite times in all the years  of working for him  and I don't know that you could call  them ordinary days because I don't know  that there's anything ordinary about  being in the president's office but I  look back with great fondness on those  days when it was just the regular  routine days in the office  and were these two very unlikely people  who based on our life experiences and  our ages we were eons apart and yet how  we came together to have a great system  of working things out and accomplishing  things and really enjoying working  together we had a very smooth running  schedule had everything all worked out  together but just this interesting  dynamic of two very unlikely people and  how we work together really well so I  treasure those days in the office of  course there were important days as well  more than others  witnessing historic signatures being  signed onto documents traveling all  across the nation and internationally  but the president was always a thrill  always private air  have to always Secret Service motorcades  you know through intersection control  pretty phenomenal but he made that a lot  of fun getting behind the scenes to see  him that was great  so post-presidency he had a very small  staff and one of my favorite jobs for  him was being his post-presidency  photographer for most of his office  stuff so here I am behind the lens of  the camera and it was great to see this  man when things were really unscripted  so did you ever think about when we have  photographs of private meetings how do  we get those photos when everybody  leaves the photographer stays so I got  to be the ultimate fly on the wall and  watch him interact with everybody from  Lady Margaret Thatcher here he is taking  her through the museum upstairs I'm sure  you guys have all either seen it or will  see it beautiful facility and it's an  honor to be here today in this beautiful  place people like Margaret Thatcher and  I also got to see them behind the scenes  after the events can you relate to this  some of you ladies in your heels yeah  the ladies have their shoes kicked off  and the president if you can see him  it's kind of got his eyes rolled in the  back of his head as if to say come on  can we just go home the end of a long  day even if this man would get tired so  how fun to be able to see them behind  the scenes like this and this looks like  an ordinary enough picture of Ronald we  can see Reagan until you realize that I  snapped this photo right as the elevator  doors were opening and Margaret Thatcher  was stepping out and if you look at  their faces they're like little kids so  happy that their friend is here very  excited and you can just see that coming  through this was a woman that they not  only had a political alliance with but  the president had a personal friendship  with and there's so much I learned about  what diplomacy looks like the Reagan way  from this man Ronald Reagan's version of  diplomacy was not political it was very  personal and it wasn't based on rhetoric  it was based on relationships and I  watched time and time again as world  leaders came to see him post presidency  you have to realize that post presidency  they didn't have to come for protocol or  diplomatic reasons when they came all  the way to Los Angeles to see Ronald  Reagan  it was because they wanted to they had a  relationship with this man that they  wanted to continue long after he was the  office holder he couldn't do anything  for them anymore politically but they  didn't care they liked him as a person  and wanted to continue that relationship  and so post presidency they came  Gorbachev came Prime Minister naka Soni  from Japan how much coal from Germany  prime minister and mrs. Brian Mulroney  from Canada a great dear friend of the  president's president like Melissa I  think symbolically they were trying to  push that wall over right he was a man  of authenticity what you saw was what  you got and as much as he loved the pomp  and circumstances of black-tie events  and going to all these fancy things he  really loved going up to the ranch it  was a man of incredible authenticity he  loved being up there at the ranch and at  have any of you been up to the ranch  it's very small it's very humble a lot  of people would picture this  presidential estate as being this  glamorous retreat it's not that at all  but Ronald Reagan loved it there he  loved taking people there he was never  embarrassed that this little tiny you  know adobe home that started its  foundation in the 1800's was where they  lived when they went up there he'd loved  taking people up there including  Margaret Thatcher he took her up there  although she doesn't look like she's  dressed for ranch activities I think  that was a dress down day for her but  you see the president in his flannel in  his jeans and his belt buckle in his  boots this is who he was he loved being  up there  he wasn't embarrassed to bring Margaret  Thatcher up to a little tiny Adobe home  up at the top of the hilltop in Santa  Barbara  he really came alive there in such a  different way he loved working outdoors  clearing brush chopping trees loved  taking his visitors around this is Brian  Mulroney seeing these two world leaders  bouncing around the back of a secret  service driven suburban was quite  enjoyable they both had Irish heritage  and had these fathers that were great  Irishmen storytellers and so the two of  them would always have this great banter  going back and forth and tell hilarious  stories and mrs. Mulroney was this great  beautiful woman and I overheard when I  was snapping the  picture she looked to mrs. Reagan and  she said Oh Nancy aren't you stylish in  your trendy bootcut jeans which were the  thing at the time and mrs. Reagan looks  at her and said these old things I've  been wearing these since the last time  these weren't in fashion so some of the  things in your closet if you hold on to  him long enough they'll come back around  by the way I was wearing a Burberry  skirt today buddy noticed that at the  beginning we can laugh at all the  clothes but ladies I was wearing  Burberry before anybody so I was ahead  not behind the fashions and when he had  Gorbachev up to the ranch here he is  driving Corbett that's actually  Gorbachev riding shotgun Ronald Reagan  is driving when Ronald Reagan was behind  the wheel of his Jeep you wanted to get  off the road because this man had not  driven in eight years you just didn't  trust his driving really ahead of his  Secret Service detail looks a little  nervous in the backseat but the  president looks like a little kid got  the steering wheel so excited to be  behind the wheel in the car and what  Gorbachev was going to be coming up to  the ranch President Reagan asked me if I  would get a gift for him and decided if  he wanted to give him a Stetson cowboy  hat now he kind of joked tongue-in-cheek  saying it may be tough to find one in  his size since he has such a big head  Gorbachev had just been named Time  magazine man of the year so I didn't  know whether he meant that figuratively  or literally but it turned into this  worldwide quest to figure out what size  cowboy had to score Bichette even where  couldn't exactly google it back in the  day I know now I sound really old  couldn't Google that so I had to stay  late into the night to call his offices  in Moscow the next morning and through  several series of phone calls and faxes  and things finally got a Russian hat  size but then had to convert it to a  Western hat size and it was all the  things I had he wondering if it would  actually work and thankfully when the  cameras were rolling and Gorbachev put  the Hat on it fit perfectly  stress I don't know if you know much  about Ronald Reagan's life story but he  talks about having very humble  beginnings here's a man who was born in  the middle of a snowstorm in the middle  of a Great Depression and a wide spot in  the road in central Illinois why all  accounts you would look at his life  story and say this guy is never gonna go  anywhere the cards were really stacked  against him his father was an alcoholic  they had no money he was in a place that  there was no opportunity and yet Ronald  Reagan was raised by an incredible  mother of faith and she taught him to be  true to his God to serve his community  to care about others she raised him to  believe that God has a plan for everyone  and seemingly random twists of fate are  all part of his plan and in the end  everything will work out for the best so  regardless of the circumstances of his  life how it appeared that nothing  possible good could come from tampico or  Dixon Illinois he was raised by a mother  who always believed that there was a  bigger plan for his life and thankfully  he believed it too now it didn't come  without a lot of hard work but he dared  to dream and he worked hard to pursue it  and he proved he's the ultimate epitome  of the American dream he proved that  anything is possible here in America so  regardless of your circumstances those  don't dictate your future and he  certainly proved that he was known for  being the Great Communicator but I would  say he was also known for being the  great optimist you guys of course don't  remember but your parents would remember  in the 1970s things in America were not  very good taxes were high inflation was  high unemployment was really high I see  a few nodding heads in the back of the  room a lot of this sound familiar here's  the beautiful part about Ronald Reagan  on day one he took office and he started  using language like it's morning in  America there's a new dawn ahead there's  a shining city on a hill and we are all  part of it in reality on Ronald Reagan's  inauguration day not one thing was  different in  America we got our hostages back that  was pretty great but the economy hadn't  changed nothing had changed our taxes  were still higher inflation was still  her employment was still high but Ronald  Reagan changed the game with optimism  and you as future leaders need to  remember optimism makes all the  difference he painted a picture for how  it could be better and people willingly  and eagerly followed him he would be the  first one to say I didn't do great  things during the 1980s  I just got government out of the way so  the people could do great things and  that's what great leaders do everything  changed with a little bit of optimism  and then a really good plan of action  that came in along behind that he was  also a man of humility so when you think  of these people on the world stage and  you think of somebody who was at the  highest pinnacle of their career  I don't know that humility is often a  word that you would think is in sync  with that but Ronald Reagan was a great  man of humility he never felt like he  was better than anybody else he felt  like he was living out his god-given  calling in life and if you were doing  the same we were all equal he kept a  sign on his desk that said there's no  limit to what a man can do or where he  can go so long as he doesn't mind who  gets the credit and that was not just  the sign on his desk that's actually the  way he lived his life there was nothing  above him or beneath him nothing was too  good for him are not good enough for him  he was just a fellow soldier on life's  path there were little ways he would  demonstrate this humility to me cute  little things like he would reach over  and hold my elbow as we would go up and  down the stairs which I don't know if  you gentlemen know that that's the  gentlemanly polite thing to do and I  always thought it was especially sweet  since I was in my 20s and he was in his  80s and he was reaching over to help me  there was another day in the office when  we had brought a bunch of extra  furniture into his office and we had a  big meeting in there and at the end I  was carrying these big heavy wooden and  leather chairs one at a time from the  conference from the office back to the  conference room and I'm one of my trips  back and forth with these big heavy  chairs I felt like somebody was  following me and I turned around and  sure enough there's Ronald Reagan right  behind  carrying one of the big heavy chairs and  I stopped and I said oh it's okay mr.  president I'll come back and get it and  he put that chair down and he stared me  down and he said what makes you think  you can carry a chair any better than I  can okay right this way we carried the  chairs together but he was a very aware  unappreciative of the people around them  and what they were doing on his behalf  he had a great sense of humor if you've  ever read a lot of his jokes and story  if he was the originator of so much of  that humor and it wasn't humor just for  the sake of humor he usually used it to  make a bigger point he also used it to  put people at ease you can imagine how I  felt meeting the president for the first  time when people came to meet him they  were obviously pretty nervous sometimes  and he would often use humor to break  the ice and to put people at ease and  make them comfortable even in big  meetings he would use stories that would  make a very significant point  well maybe using a little humor to do it  so as leaders we need to realize that  humor is an important component now I'm  not saying go run out and buy a joke  book or anything but I'm saying you can  take the role seriously without taking  yourself too seriously Ronald Reagan was
 

